<h1>Prompt-Injected Memory: Summaries as Compressed State</h1>
<p>An LLM has no memory between requests — every "conversation" is a replay.
Long-term memory is therefore an engineering choice about <em>what to replay</em>.</p>

<h2>Three tiers of remembering</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>The context window</strong> (built in): replay recent turns
verbatim. This game keeps 10 turns per NPC — beyond that, cost and small-model
attention degrade.</li>
<li><strong>The summary</strong> (this lesson): compress everything older into
a few sentences and inject them into the system prompt. Cheap, bounded,
survives restarts once persisted.</li>
<li><strong>Retrieval (RAG)</strong>: store everything, embed, fetch relevant
pieces per query. Powerful, but a whole retrieval stack for a hot-dog vendor
is not v1 engineering.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Who writes the memory? The NPC does</h2>
<p>When a conversation closes, the game asks the NPC's own model to update its
note — first person, three sentences, "who is this player, what happened,
what should I remember." First person matters: the summary re-enters the
prompt as the character's own recollection, so voice and perspective survive
the round-trip. Marge remembers "Sam loves my rye" — not "the user expressed
a preference for rye bread."</p>
<pre><code>// On conversation close (main.cpp):
//   transcript -> "update your note" request -> NPC's model
// On reply:
//   npc.setMemory(note); store.save(name, note, history)
// On next launch:
//   npc.setMemory(store.load(name).summary)</code></pre>

<h2>Placement in the prompt is load-bearing</h2>
<p><code>renderSystemPrompt(memory)</code> inserts the memory <em>before</em>
the action/mood protocol, not at the end. Small local models weight trailing
instructions most heavily — the directive contract must stay last or tag
compliance drops. Prompt sections are ordered by how badly you need them
obeyed, back to front.</p>

<h2>Persistence: boring on purpose</h2>
<p>One SQLite table: <code>conversations(npc_id, summary, transcript_json,
updated_at)</code>. The summary is the working state; the transcript is for
debugging ("why does Marge think my name is Kevin?"). Failure semantics are
the important design: a missing database, unwritable path, or corrupt row
degrades to a memoryless session with a logged warning — memory is an
enhancement, and an enhancement is never allowed to crash the game.</p>
<div class="callout warn">Summarization drift is the known failure mode: each
compression loses detail, and errors compound ("Sam mentioned bread" becomes
"Sam is a baker" three summaries later). Mitigations: feed the previous note
into the update request (the model corrects rather than re-invents), keep
notes short (less room to confabulate), and keep transcripts for auditing.</div>

<h2>Multiplayer wrinkle</h2>
<p>Memories belong to the host's world — guests talk to the host's NPCs, so
their exploits land in the host's saves. Replicating memories to guests would
mean replicating identity; that's a design question for later, not an
accident to stumble into.</p>
<div class="callout good">Takeaway: "long-term memory" for an LLM character is
state compression with a persistence layer — the model is both the consumer
and the compressor, and the prompt is the only bus that carries it back in.</div>
